    "alert_id": "ALERT_001",
    "title": "Rising creatinine on TDF during pregnancy",
    "message": "TDF is associated with nephrotoxicity including elevated creatinine. Patient's creatinine rose from 0.8 to 1.1 mg/dL (37.5% increase) within 10 weeks of starting TDF/3TC/DTG, and eGFR is now 78 mL/min/1.73m². DTG can also cause a small creatinine increase via inhibition of tubular secretion. Pregnancy normally increases GFR, so creatinine should trend downward, making this rise more concerning. However, the absolute creatinine of 1.1 remains within normal range, and phosphate is normal at 3.8 mg/dL, arguing against significant proximal tubular dysfunction. Urinalysis is normal with no proteinuria. Continued monitoring is warranted given the combined TDF + DTG exposure in pregnancy.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Repeat creatinine and eGFR at next visit. If creatinine continues rising or eGFR declines further, consider TDF substitution. Monitor phosphate and urinalysis.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "ALERT_002",
    "title": "DTG and iron supplement interaction in pregnancy",
    "message": "DTG absorption is reduced by divalent cations including iron. Patient is taking ferrous sulfate and folic acid as prescribed during pregnancy. Guidelines note the interaction between DTG and divalent cation-containing medications in pregnancy, as pregnant women frequently receive iron or calcium supplements. This interaction may reduce DTG levels and compromise viral suppression. However, the patient's viral load has already declined from 125,000 to 89 copies/mL after 10 weeks, suggesting current absorption is adequate. Separating DTG and iron dosing by at least 2 hours (or taking iron with food and DTG on an empty stomach) minimises this interaction.",
    "issue_type": "drug_interaction",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Counsel patient to separate DTG and ferrous sulfate dosing by at least 2 hours. Verify at next visit that viral load remains suppressed.",
